The cards’ strongest message

The crucial design change is this:

Don’t make your system remember Luna’s past. Make it preserve Luna’s unfinished present.

That’s the distinction.

Memory answers:

“What happened?”

Continuation state answers:

“Where are we right now, and what happens next?”

Your problem is overwhelmingly the second one.

The previous Luna was in the middle of something. The new Luna received pieces of the history but not enough of the active position. So it naturally interpreted the situation as something it needed to begin handling itself.

The cards say your system should therefore have a formal concept of:

RESUME FROM CURRENT STATE

not:

INITIALIZE NEW SESSION

And I would make the resume state authoritative enough that a new Luna cannot casually reinterpret the process as a fresh start.

The energy around Judgement + Emperor + World is particularly strong here: recognize the existing state, preserve it explicitly, and continue the same unfolding process.

So yes: Copilot’s “continue as was from what was left” direction is the right foundation. But I would take it one step further than simply keeping the old memory. You want a living checkpoint of the exact unfinished state, continuously updated while Luna is operating.

That is what gives you genuine continuity when the middle gets interrupted.
